
<p>Joe Rossignol, on Thursday:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Starting today, iPhone users still running iOS 26 will see iOS
27.0.1 as the prominent software update in the Settings app under
General → Software Update, with iOS 26.7.1 now relegated to a
smaller “Also Available” section at the bottom.</p>
</blockquote>

<p><a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/10/08/apple-is-slow-rolling-ios-27-adoption-so-far">Just the other day</a> I posted about how most users only get OS updates when Apple pushes them through automatic updates. But even for users who update manually, until Thursday, the default suggested update for users on iOS 26.x was to remain on the latest version of iOS 26. To get iOS 27 you had to scroll down “below the fold” and most users don’t do that.</p>

<p>iOS 27 adoption amongst normals is very slow, but that’s only because Apple wants it slow. That’s not because iOS 27 is buggy — it speaks to its Snow-Leopard-like focus on bug fixes and performance updates that it’s actually remarkably stable. The obvious explanation is that Apple slow-rolling 27 updates to gate access to Siri AI.</p>

